\section{Grouping identity}{

\code{set_id} is the one-based Grouping set identifier exposed by \code{.id} for the
same resolved plan. \code{grouping_bits} instead describes whether each variable
dimension is included (\code{0L}) or omitted (\code{1L}), and \code{grouping_id} encodes
that absence pattern as a bit mask. \code{grouping_id} is \code{NA_integer_} beyond
31 variable dimensions, while \code{grouping_bits} remains complete.

\code{\link[=grouping_bit]{grouping_bit()}} is the canonical reference for how these four values
relate and which of them distinguish duplicate grouping-set occurrences.
}

\section{Formats and ordinary tibble behavior}{

The default \code{.format = "text"} represents column collections as \verb{()},
\code{(region)}, or \verb{(region, store)}, and Grouping bits as text such as
\verb{region=0, store=1}. This compact display is the default because IDE table
viewers, including Positron, commonly hide list-column contents.

\code{.format = "list"} returns exact character vectors for \code{fixed}, \code{included},
and \code{omitted}, plus named integer vectors for \code{grouping_bits}. Use it for
programmatic inspection and for names containing separators. The text
format is display-only and is not a serialization format.

The result has only the ordinary \code{tbl_df}, \code{tbl}, and \code{data.frame} classes,
has no custom printer, and works with normal dplyr, export, and
snapshot-testing workflows. Its rows are always in Grouping-plan order.
That inspection guarantee does not imply physical result order for a Margin
operation.

A lazy \code{.data} still returns a local tibble without reading source rows:
inspection uses captured column metadata and the backend-independent
Grouping plan. It is deliberately separate from a SQL execution plan. Use
\code{\link[dplyr:show_query]{dplyr::show_query()}} for generated SQL and backend-native tools for an
optimizer plan.

Because inspection builds no grouping-set branch, it also accepts a dtplyr
step rooted at \code{dtplyr::lazy_dt(immutable = FALSE)} and leaves the caller's
\code{data.table} unchanged. Margin verbs refuse the same Mutable step before
building a branch; rebuild it with \code{immutable = TRUE} before passing it to
one of them.

The \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/grouping_identity.html}{grouping identity guide} walks through inspecting a plan and
reading it back off a Margin result. The \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/recipes.html}{recipes guide} uses a
plan to choose which grouping set to keep, and joins one against \code{.id} to
name the set behind every row. Because the plan is local, that join needs a
\code{copy} argument when the Margin result is lazy, and it does not preserve a
Margin order.
